    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Painted Grey Ware is an archaeologists’ name for a culture identified by its fine grey bowls painted with simple black patterns, found at 1,576 sites in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</li><li>Its people farmed with the plough, kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, sheep, pigs and horses, worked iron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, and lived mostly in villages, with several dozen towns.</li><li>It probably corresponds to the middle and late Vedic period and the Kuru-Panchala kingdom, and many of its settlements grew into the towns of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-black-polished-ware">Northern Black Polished Ware</a> period.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The painted grey ware was a luxury: almost all of it is drinking ware, and it makes up only 10–15% of the pottery found from Vedic times, most of the rest being red ware.</p></aside>
    </section>

